entry geiger-counter
term: Geiger Counter
image: ../../assets/images/terms/geiger counter.webp
related_terms: radiation
related_terms: dosimeter
related_terms: dose-rate
related_terms: ionizing-radiation
quick_facts: Counts ionization pulses from a gas-filled tube
quick_facts: Geiger–Müller design dates to 1928
quick_facts: Detects alpha (thin window), beta, and gamma
quick_facts: Reports counts — not energy or full dose by itself
quick_facts: Cheap, rugged, and instantly readable

sources:
    {"organization":"EPA","title":"Radiation Detection and Measurement","url":"https://www.epa.gov/radiation"}

definition:
    A portable radiation detector built around a gas-filled tube: when ionizing radiation passes through, it triggers a brief electrical avalanche counted as one click or blip. Invented by Hans Geiger in 1908 and refined with Walther Müller in 1928 into the rugged Geiger–Müller tube used everywhere from power plants to prospecting. With the right window it detects alpha, beta, and gamma radiation — though it only counts events, never telling you their energy.

debunking_snippet:
    “If the clicking stops, it's safe.” Dangerous trap: in extreme fields a Geiger tube saturates and goes quiet while radiation is at its worst. Silence is not safety.
